.BS
.SH NAME
ns_critsec \- Operate on critical section objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_critsec create\fR
.sp
\fBns_critsec destroy\fR \fIcsid\fR
.sp
\fBns_critsec enter\fR \fIcsid\fR
.sp
\fBns_critsec eval\fR \fIcsid\fR \fIscript\fR
.sp
\fBns_critsec leave\fR \fIcsid\fR
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBns_critsec\fR command provides a mechanism for creating and
managing critical section objects, which are used for synchronizing
access to shared resources in a multithreaded environment\&. Critical
sections ensure that only one thread can access a protected code
section at a time, preventing data corruption and ensuring thread
safety\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_critsec create\fR
Initializes a new critical section object and returns a handle
(\fIcsid\fR) to it\&. This handle is used in other \fBns_critsec\fR commands
to reference the critical section\&.
.TP
\fBns_critsec destroy\fR \fIcsid\fR
Destroys the critical section object identified by \fIcsid\fR and
frees any resources it was using\&. NOTE: In the current
implementation, this command is effectively a no-op; the critical
section will remain until the server shuts down\&.
.TP
\fBns_critsec enter\fR \fIcsid\fR
Enters the critical section \fIcsid\fR\&.  The thread will block if another thread
is already in the critical section\&.
Enters the critical section identified by \fIcsid\fR\&. The actual
thread will block other threads trying to enter the same critical
section\&. If \fIcsid\fR was not created beforehand, it is
auto-created (not recommended for best practices)\&.
.TP
\fBns_critsec eval\fR \fIcsid\fR \fIscript\fR
Evaluates the provided \fIscript\fR inside the critical section
identified by \fIcsid\fR\&. This command ensures the script runs with
exclusive access to the critical section\&. If \fIcsid\fR was not created
beforehand, it is auto-created (not recommended for best practices)\&.
.TP
\fBns_critsec leave\fR \fIcsid\fR
Leaves the critical section identified by \fIcsid\fR\&. If a thread has
entered the critical section multiple times, it must call \fIcsid\fR as
many times as it entered\&. When a thread fully exits the critical
section, a notification is sent to other threads waiting on the same
critical section\&.
.PP
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates the usage of \fBns_critsec\fR
commands to safely access and modify a shared resource:
.CS


 #
 # Create a critical section and store its handle
 #
 nsv_set \&. log_critsec [ns_critsec create]

 proc log_message {message} {
    set critsec [nsv_get \&. log_critsec]
    #
    # Enter the critical section to ensure only one thread writes at a time
    #
    ns_critsec enter $critsec
    set timestamp [clock format [clock seconds] -format "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"]
    set handle [open "shared_log\&.txt" "a"]
    puts $handle "$timestamp - $message"
    close $handle
    ns_critsec leave $critsec
 }

 #
 # Example of multiple threads writing to the log file
 # (assuming, the proc "log_message" was loaded before and is
 # available in all threads)
 #
 ns_thread create -detached { log_message "Thread 1: Starting process" }
 ns_thread create -detached { log_message "Thread 2: Completed task A" }
 ns_thread create -detached { log_message "Thread 3: Encountered an error" }

.CE
Explanation:
.IP \(bu
The \fIlog_message\fR procedure ensures that only one thread at a time writes to the shared log file\&.
.IP \(bu
This prevents overlapping or incomplete log entries and ensures log data integrity\&.
.IP \(bu
Each thread can safely call \fIlog_message\fR, and the critical section guarantees exclusive access during the write operation\&.
.PP
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ns_cond, ns_mutex, ns_rwlock, ns_sema, ns_thread
.SH KEYWORDS
concurrency, server built-in, synchronization, threading
